INT. NIGHT BUS 47 - MOVING - NIGHT

Near-empty. Overhead lights hum. Rain drags sideways across the windows. Wipers tick.

ELDON MCPHAIL, 50s, driver, hat low, one hand on the wheel and the other around a cold thermos. In the mirror: two passengers.

SORAYA KHALILI, 30s, backpack on her lap, sits halfway back, glued to her phone. Behind her, BENNETT OLUMIDE, 40s, in a damp raincoat, stares out at the passing dark.

Eldon clears his throat.

ELDON
(to the bus)
Next stop, Harbor and Fifth. Mind the gap.

Nobody reacts.

ELDON (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Nobody ever minds the gap.

Soraya looks up.

SORAYA
Is this the 47?

ELDON
It's the 47.

SORAYA
It's not going to Eastgate?

ELDON
Not tonight.

SORAYA
Since when?

ELDON
Since the flood.

Soraya sighs. Pockets her phone. Bennett turns his head, slowly.

BENNETT
You're the third bus tonight.

ELDON
Third what?

BENNETT
Third driver who didn't say where we're going.

ELDON
I said Harbor and Fifth.

BENNETT
And after that?

ELDON
After that, nothing. Last stop.

Bennett nods, slowly, as though it explains something he's been wondering about.

SORAYA
(to Eldon)
Can you let me off before Harbor?

ELDON
Not an official stop.

SORAYA
I'll pay extra.

ELDON
It's not about the fare, miss.

SORAYA
Then what is it about?

ELDON
Rules.

SORAYA
(muttering)
Great.

The bus slows. Brakes sigh. The doors fold open to rain.

ELDON
Harbor and Fifth.

Soraya stands. Bennett doesn't.

SORAYA
Are you coming?

BENNETT
No.

SORAYA
This is your stop.

BENNETT
It's his stop.

He nods at Eldon.

ELDON
(to the empty bus)
Everybody out, please.

Soraya freezes. Looks at Bennett. At Eldon. At the empty seats behind them.

SORAYA
(whisper)
Who is he talking to?

BENNETT
The ones who stayed.

Eldon closes the doors. The wipers resume. The bus pulls out, carrying all three of them into the dark.
